After simplification, we still have one indeterminate form of $\frac{0}{0}$ given that $\sin(x)-x=0$ and $\sin(x)\to 0$ when $x\to 0$. Thus applying L'Hopital's theorem \begin {align*}
 \lim\limits_{\theta\to 0}  \frac{1}{\frac{d }{dx}(\sin(x)) - \frac{d}{dx}(x)}
\end{align*}
There are known derivatives for sine and x, namely $\frac{d }{dx}(\sin(x))$ = $ \cos(x)$ and $\frac{d}{dx}(x)=1$

